      "card_ids": ["C241","C260","C254"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Other tricyclics with the same dimethylaminopropylidene side chain also show high first-pass liability: doxepin has an in vivo intrinsic clearance of 223 ml/min/kg, and nortriptyline shows near-unity hepatic extraction in perfused rat liver and clearance near liver blood flow.",
      "card_ids": ["C227","C253","C270"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 records show high intestinal absorption and no significant efflux for this class (chlorpromazine 96% oral absorption and BCS class I; imipramine efflux ratio ~1.1), so oral F is governed by hepatic first-pass rather than absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C146","C178","C214","C207"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The L1 high-F contexts (trazodone, alprazolam, clobazam, glibenclamide, 2,4-D, triazolam) are structurally distant from the query and do not provide affirmative evidence against its first-pass liability.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C08","C10","C28"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "changed",
      "card_ids": ["C241","C260","C254","C227","C253","C270"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability or first-pass measurement for chlorprothixene itself.",
    "L6 evidence is analog-based (chlorpromazine, doxepin, nortriptyline) rather than query-specific; species and assay-system differences limit quantitative transfer.",
    "No direct human hepatic extraction or oral AUC/Cmax data for the query.",
    "Query solubility/dissolution is not directly measured; high logP may affect absorption, though the basic amine may form salts.",
    "L1 high-F contexts are structurally distant and do not resolve the query's first-pass question."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L6 hepatic-clearance evidence from the closest analogs (chlorpromazine, doxepin, nortriptyline) shows high intrinsic clearance/extraction, indicating substantial first-pass loss for this tricyclic thioxanthene class. With high absorption and no efflux barrier, F is limited by hepatic first-pass and is better supported as <20%. The structurally distant L1 high-F contexts do not override this mechanistic evidence. Prediction flipped to low with moderate confidence."
